"""Author topic-aligned Reading and Writing Skills quizzes and validated answer keys."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,random
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
OUT=ROOT/'output/reading-writing-quizzes'
TOPICS=[
('Text as Connected Discourse',r"""
Which feature most clearly makes a set of sentences connected discourse?|The sentences develop a shared purpose through related ideas.|Every sentence has exactly the same length.|Every sentence begins with a transition word.|The text contains several different topics without explanation.|Connected discourse depends on meaningful relationships among ideas, purpose, and audience.
Read: 'Bring a clean bottle. Fill it before the trip.' What does 'it' refer to?|The bottle|The trip|The instruction|The reader|The singular pronoun refers back to the bottle that can be filled.
A paragraph explains how to return library books, then suddenly lists basketball scores. Which property is most directly weakened?|Coherence|Spelling|Verb tense|Capitalization|The unrelated scores interrupt the paragraph's central purpose and logical unity.
Which revision makes the reference clearest? 'Ana gave Bea her notes before the test.'|Before the test, Ana gave Bea the notes Ana had written.|Before the test, she gave her her notes.|Ana gave Bea those things before it.|Her notes were given to her before the test.|Naming the writer of the notes removes the ambiguous reference of 'her.'
Which planning method is most suitable for generating many possible ideas before selecting them?|Brainstorming|Proofreading|Formatting the final bibliography|Submitting a finished draft|Brainstorming produces possibilities before ideas are evaluated and organized.
A writer must show the stages of a book-return procedure. Which organizer best fits?|A sequence chart|An unrelated word list|A family tree of the writer's relatives|A chart of word lengths|A sequence chart makes the order of procedural stages visible.
Which entry belongs in a topic outline rather than a sentence outline?|Benefits of regular reading|Regular reading can broaden vocabulary.|Readers encounter new ideas in books.|A daily routine supports reading practice.|A topic outline uses words or phrases; a sentence outline uses complete sentences.
Which pair of supporting points is parallel under 'Preparing the study space'?|Checking the lighting; arranging the materials|Checking the lighting; the materials are ready|The light; arranging things is helpful|To check the lighting; materials were arranged|Both supporting points use the same grammatical form and support one main idea.
Read: 'The pathway was flooded. Therefore, the class used another entrance.' What relationship does 'therefore' signal?|A result|An example|A contrast|A definition|The second sentence states a result of the flooding.
A school advisory needs an event time, but the writer has not confirmed it. What is the best action?|Verify the time before including it.|Invent a likely time to complete the advisory.|Omit every other event detail as well.|Add several possible times without explaining them.|A useful advisory must give reliable information rather than unsupported details.
"""),
('Patterns of Development',r"""
A paragraph recounts a learner's first day at a new school in time order. Which pattern dominates?|Narration|Definition|Classification|Comparison and contrast|Narration develops a sequence of events, often in chronological order.
A passage describes the library's narrow aisles, bright windows, and quiet reading corners. Which pattern dominates?|Description|Problem and solution|Persuasion|Classification|Description develops a subject through selected details of its features.
Which sentence begins a definition of a peer tutor most effectively?|A peer tutor is a learner who helps another learner understand a subject.|Our peer tutor arrived at eight yesterday.|The peer tutor wore a blue jacket.|We should invite more peer tutors tomorrow.|The sentence identifies a general category and distinguishing function.
A writer explains the value of revision by discussing three actual improvements made to a draft. Which pattern is being used?|Exemplification|Narration without examples|Spatial description|Classification by age|Specific examples illustrate and support the general point about revision.
Which plan best uses classification?|Group reading-club participants by grade level using the same rule for everyone.|Describe one book's cover in detail.|Retell how one learner found a book.|Argue that one novel deserves an award.|Classification groups members of a larger category according to a consistent basis.
Which plan best develops comparison and contrast?|Discuss printed and digital texts using the same criteria: access, portability, and annotation.|Describe a printed book, then discuss unrelated school rules.|List only the titles of two books.|Explain only how one text was printed.|Using common criteria reveals meaningful similarities and differences.
A paragraph explains how repeated practice can improve reading fluency. Which relationship should it establish?|Cause and effect|A definition based only on appearance|An alphabetical arrangement|A contrast between unrelated subjects|Cause and effect connects a condition or action to its consequences.
A school has low participation in a reading club. Which outline best follows problem and solution?|Explain the participation problem, propose a response, and assess its feasibility.|List every school club without discussing participation.|Describe the club room without proposing any response.|Narrate a sports event unrelated to reading.|Problem and solution identifies a difficulty and develops a justified response.
A letter asks the school to extend library hours and supports the request with usage records. Which pattern best describes its main purpose?|Persuasion|Description of physical appearance|Narration of a fictional event|Classification by book size|The letter aims to convince a decision maker through reasons and evidence.
Which sentence most clearly signals contrast?|Unlike the printed guide, the digital guide can be searched by keyword.|The guide was printed on Monday.|For example, the guide has a glossary.|As a result, the guide was revised.|'Unlike' explicitly introduces a difference between comparable items.
A writer argues that online reading is always better because two friends prefer it. What would most improve the persuasive development?|Use relevant evidence and acknowledge conditions in which each format helps.|Repeat the preference in every paragraph.|Replace reasons with insults toward print readers.|Remove the audience and purpose from the discussion.|Persuasive writing needs support suited to the scope of the claim and awareness of limitations.
A paragraph both describes a flooded classroom and explains its causes. How should its pattern be identified?|Identify its main purpose and explain how the supporting pattern contributes.|Assume a text can contain only one development pattern.|Call it narration solely because it mentions a classroom.|Classify it by the number of sentences instead of its organization.|Texts may combine patterns; the dominant pattern follows the writer's main purpose.
"""),
('Properties of Effective Writing',r"""
Which property concerns the logical arrangement of a text's main and supporting ideas?|Organization|Font size|Paper color|Number of adjectives|Organization concerns the structure and sequence through which ideas are developed.
A paragraph has a clear main idea but shifts between unrelated reasons without explanation. Which revision best improves coherence?|Arrange related reasons together and explain their connections.|Insert 'however' at the start of every sentence.|Replace all nouns with pronouns.|Increase the font size.|Coherence improves when readers can follow logical relationships among ideas.
Which revision improves cohesion between these sentences? 'The library extended its hours. The change helped working learners.'|The library extended its hours. This change helped working learners.|The library extended its hours. That person helped working learners.|The library extended its hours. Nevertheless, because, therefore.|The library extended its hours. Blue tables are expensive.|'This change' refers clearly to the extension of library hours.
Which sentence is most appropriate in a formal request to a principal?|We respectfully request permission to conduct a peer-reading activity.|Hey, let us do this thing, okay?|You must approve this because we said so.|This plan is, like, totally awesome.|The sentence makes a clear request using language suitable for the audience.
Which sentence has correct subject-verb agreement?|The list of reading materials is on the desk.|The list of reading materials are on the desk.|The lists of reading material is on the desk.|The list of reading materials be on the desk.|The singular subject 'list' requires the singular verb 'is.'
Which item is a sentence fragment?|Because the library closed early.|The library closed early.|We returned the books before noon.|The librarian checked the returned books.|The dependent clause begins with 'because' and lacks an independent clause.
Which revision corrects the comma splice? 'The draft was unclear, the writer revised it.'|The draft was unclear, so the writer revised it.|The draft was unclear the writer revised it.|The draft, was unclear the writer revised it.|The draft was, unclear, the writer revised it.|A comma plus the coordinating conjunction 'so' correctly joins the independent clauses.
Which sentence is the most concise while keeping the meaning?|The class revised the article to improve clarity.|The class made a revision of the article for the purpose of making its clarity better.|In order to improve clarity, a revision was made by the class of the article.|The class made the article more clear in terms of its clarity.|The direct sentence removes unnecessary nominalizations and repeated ideas.
A formal report says, 'The activity was a huge success,' but gives no results. What is the most useful revision?|State specific outcomes and the evidence supporting the evaluation.|Add more exclamation marks.|Replace 'huge' with an even stronger adjective.|Repeat the statement in the conclusion without evidence.|Specific, supported language is clearer and more credible than vague praise.
Which sequence best distinguishes revising from proofreading?|First improve ideas and organization; then check grammar, spelling, and punctuation.|First change the font; then remove all supporting ideas.|Treat revising and proofreading as checking spelling only.|Submit immediately after adding a title.|Revision addresses content and structure; proofreading checks surface accuracy afterward.
"""),
('Critical Reading and Claims',r"""
What does critical reading primarily require?|Examining a text's claims, reasoning, evidence, and context.|Rejecting every statement in a text.|Agreeing with a writer who uses formal language.|Reading only the title and conclusion.|Critical reading evaluates how a text constructs and supports meaning.
Read: 'The school should provide a quiet reading area.' What kind of claim is this?|A claim of policy|A claim of fact reporting a measured result|A definition of a reading area|A narrative detail without a recommendation|The word 'should' proposes a course of action.
Which statement is a claim of value?|Accessible libraries are an important part of an inclusive school.|The library has 24 chairs according to its inventory.|The reading activity began at nine.|The school should buy more chairs next month.|The statement makes an evaluative judgment about importance.
Which statement is a claim of fact?|The attendance register records 38 participants in the session.|Reading is the most enjoyable activity of all.|The school ought to hold a reading session weekly.|A reading room is more beautiful than a laboratory.|A claim of fact can be checked against evidence such as a register.
Read: 'Every seat was occupied, and several learners stood at the back.' Which inference is most reasonable?|The room did not have enough seats for everyone present.|The learners disliked reading.|The school had no other rooms anywhere.|Every learner arrived late.|The details support an inference about seating, but not motives or wider school conditions.
An advertisement says, 'Real achievers choose our review course.' Which assumption should a critical reader question?|That taking the course is necessary to count as an achiever.|That the sentence contains a product name.|That all review courses take place on Saturdays.|That every course provides free books.|The wording links achievement and identity to buying the advertised course without establishing the connection.
Which question best checks the context of a news report?|When was it published, who produced it, and what event or evidence does it address?|Does its headline contain my favorite word?|Is its background color attractive?|Can I repeat its conclusion without reading it?|Authorship, date, purpose, and circumstances help readers interpret a report responsibly.
A proposal says, 'Our survey proves all learners prefer audio lessons,' but surveyed only five club members. What is the main concern?|The evidence is too limited to support a claim about all learners.|Surveys can never provide useful information.|Audio lessons cannot be discussed in writing.|The claim is reliable because it includes the word 'proves.'|A small, selected group cannot establish a universal preference for all learners.
Which response best distinguishes an explicit claim from an implied claim?|An explicit claim is directly stated; an implied claim is inferred from textual details.|An explicit claim is always true; an implied claim is always false.|An explicit claim has no author; an implied claim names its author.|An explicit claim appears only in the title.|The distinction concerns how the text communicates a claim, not whether the claim is true.
Two reports describe the same activity differently. What should a critical reader do first?|Compare their evidence, scope, dates, and possible purposes.|Choose whichever report is longer.|Assume both reports are false.|Accept whichever title sounds more confident.|Comparing relevant evidence and context is more reliable than using length or confidence alone.
"""),
('Assertions, Counterclaims, and Evidence',r"""
Which statement is a focused assertion that could guide a short argument?|A weekly peer-reading session can support reading practice when learners receive appropriate guidance.|Reading, schools, and many other things exist.|This is a very interesting topic about everything.|Everyone knows all activities are always effective.|The assertion states a specific, qualified position that evidence can support or challenge.
A writer recommends weekly reading sessions. Which response is a relevant counterclaim?|Weekly sessions may reduce time for other lessons unless the timetable is adjusted.|The library walls were painted green.|The writer's surname is difficult to pronounce.|Some books have illustrated covers.|A counterclaim challenges the position through a relevant concern about its effects.
Which response treats a counterclaim most fairly?|State the opposing concern accurately, then address it with reasons and evidence.|Misrepresent the concern so it is easier to dismiss.|Insult the person who raised it.|Pretend that no reasonable objection could exist.|A fair response engages with the actual objection rather than distorting it.
A claim concerns whether reading sessions improved fluency. Which evidence is most directly relevant?|Comparable fluency measurements taken before and after the sessions.|The color of the session posters.|The facilitator's favorite novel.|The number of windows in the room.|Fluency measurements directly relate to the claimed outcome, although study limitations still matter.
Which evidence would most strengthen a claim about attendance at one reading event?|The event's dated attendance register, checked for duplicate entries.|An unrelated learner's guess.|The number of likes on an old sports post.|A statement that attendance felt high.|A checked record of the specific event is more direct than impressions or unrelated popularity.
A writer selects only favorable results and omits several unfavorable results from the same activity. What should be improved?|Present the relevant results fairly and explain limitations.|Change the unfavorable results to favorable ones.|Use a larger title to make the argument convincing.|Remove the source of every result.|An evidence-based argument should not selectively hide results that affect its conclusion.
Which sentence best introduces a rebuttal to a concern about timetable conflicts?|Although scheduling is a concern, a voluntary lunchtime session would avoid replacing regular lessons.|Only foolish people worry about schedules.|Schedules have nothing to do with school activities.|The concern is wrong because I dislike it.|The rebuttal acknowledges the concern and offers a relevant response.
A report finds that frequent readers also have higher vocabulary scores. Which conclusion is best justified by this finding alone?|Reading frequency and vocabulary scores were associated in the reported group.|Reading frequency definitely caused every vocabulary gain.|Every nonreader has a low vocabulary score.|The relationship must apply to every learner in the country.|An association alone does not establish causation or justify universal conclusions.
Which practice best supports responsible use of evidence from a source?|Represent the source accurately, identify it, and distinguish its findings from your interpretation.|Copy its wording without credit because the argument is useful.|Change its numbers to match your position.|Cite its title without checking what it says.|Responsible evidence use requires accurate representation and attribution.
Which conclusion is most appropriately qualified?|The results suggest that the activity helped the participating learners, but a broader study is needed.|The activity will solve every reading difficulty in every school.|No further evidence could ever change this conclusion.|Every learner who missed the activity cannot improve.|A qualified conclusion matches the available evidence and recognizes its scope.
"""),
('Academic and Professional Writing',r"""
A learner must explain what a proposed reading project is, why it matters, and how it will work. Which document best fits?|A concept paper|A personal shopping list|A fictional diary entry|A weather bulletin|A concept paper develops a proposed idea, its purpose, and its significance.
Which section of a project proposal identifies the resources and estimated costs needed?|Budget|Salutation|Complimentary close|Personal signature|A proposal budget sets out needed resources and estimated expenses.
Which objective is most specific and measurable?|Hold four peer-reading sessions for 20 registered participants during November.|Make all learners perfect readers immediately.|Do something useful about reading soon.|Create the best project in the world.|The objective identifies an action, quantity, audience, and time frame.
Which information most directly supports the rationale of a reading project proposal?|Evidence of the target learners' reading needs and an explanation of how the project addresses them.|The project leader's favorite color.|The length of the proposal's title.|A list of unrelated celebrations.|The rationale explains the need for the project and the relevance of its response.
A report must summarize an activity that has already happened. Which detail is most appropriate?|Verified dates, participant counts, activities, outcomes, and limitations.|Invented outcomes that would please the reader.|Only plans that have not yet been carried out.|Unsupported praise without details.|A report records and interprets what occurred using reliable information.
Which opening is most suitable for a formal permission letter?|I am writing to request permission to conduct a peer-reading session on the proposed date.|You have to let us do this right now.|Hey, approve our stuff whenever.|Guess what we are doing without permission.|A formal opening states the request clearly and respectfully.
Which practice makes a formal request actionable?|State the request, relevant arrangements, and a reliable way to contact the sender.|Use only vague phrases such as 'that thing.'|Omit the sender's identity and contact details.|Replace the request with unrelated compliments.|The recipient needs enough relevant information to understand and respond to the request.
Which résumé entry presents an experience most responsibly?|Assisted with weekly library inventory, recorded returned books, and organized shelf labels.|Claimed to manage the entire library although only visiting once.|Listed an unearned certificate to appear qualified.|Invented an employment history to fill space.|A résumé should accurately describe actual experience and responsibilities.
What is the main purpose of an application letter accompanying a résumé?|Connect relevant qualifications to the opportunity and explain the applicant's interest.|Repeat every résumé detail without considering the role.|Guarantee selection by exaggerating achievements.|Discuss unrelated personal disputes.|An application letter explains the fit between the applicant and the opportunity.
A critique of an article should mainly include which combination?|An accurate account of its main position and an evaluation supported by reasons and evidence.|Only praise for the author's reputation.|A rewritten title followed by unsupported insults.|A list of spelling mistakes without discussing the argument.|A critique combines understanding of the work with a reasoned evaluation.
Which revision improves the professional tone of a workplace email?|Could you confirm whether the revised schedule is available by Friday?|Send that thing now!!!|Why are you always so useless with schedules?|I need stuff, okay?|The revision uses a clear, courteous request and identifies the required information.
A proposal predicts 30 participants, while the activity report records 24 actual participants. What should the report do?|Report 24 as the actual count and explain the difference when relevant.|Report 30 so it matches the proposal.|Average the numbers and present 27 as the actual count.|Omit all counts because the estimate was different.|Reports distinguish verified outcomes from earlier estimates rather than altering results.
""")]

def build():
    OUT.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    guides=json.loads((ROOT/'output/reading-writing-assessments/assessment-manifest.json').read_text(encoding='utf-8'))
    quizzes=[];keys=['# Reading and Writing Skills — Answer Keys','']
    rng=random.Random(20261024)
    for number,(title,block) in enumerate(TOPICS,1):
        guide=guides[number-1];assert guide['topicTitle']==title
        rows=[line.split('|') for line in block.strip().splitlines()]
        expected=12 if number in [2,6] else 10
        assert len(rows)==expected,(title,len(rows))
        targets=[i%4 for i in range(expected)];rng.shuffle(targets)
        questions=[];keys.extend([f'## {number}. {title}', ''])
        for index,(row,target) in enumerate(zip(rows,targets),1):
            assert len(row)==6,(title,index,row)
            prompt,answer,*rest=row;distractors,explanation=rest[:3],rest[3]
            assert len(set([answer]+distractors))==4
            rng.shuffle(distractors);choices=distractors[:];choices.insert(target,answer)
            questions.append(dict(id=f'RW-T{number:02}-Q{index:02}',type='Multiple Choice',question=prompt,choices=choices,answer=answer,answerIndex=target,points=1,explanation=explanation))
            keys.append(f'{index}. **{chr(65+target)} — {answer}** {explanation}')
        quiz=dict(id=f'RW-11-12-QUIZ-T{number:02}',code=f'#QZ-RW-{number:02}',title=title+' Quiz',description=f'Reading and Writing Skills: {title}. Choose the best answer for each question. Each correct answer earns 1 point.',status='published',questions=questions,totalQuestions=len(questions),totalPoints=len(questions),timeLimit=20 if expected==12 else 15,isAi=True,icon='fa-solid fa-clipboard-question',color='green',topicId=guide['topicId'],quarter=guide['quarter'],competencyCodes=guide['codes'])
        quizzes.append(quiz);keys.append('')
    assert sum(q['totalQuestions'] for q in quizzes)==64
    (OUT/'quizzes.json').write_text(json.dumps(quizzes,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf-8')
    (OUT/'answer-keys.md').write_text('\n'.join(keys),encoding='utf-8')
    print(json.dumps({'quizzes':len(quizzes),'questions':sum(q['totalQuestions'] for q in quizzes),'counts':[q['totalQuestions'] for q in quizzes]}))
if __name__=='__main__':build()
